
User prompt: Please apply the W3C Recharter Review skill to https://www.w3.org/2026/11/proposed-atag-wg-charter.html
Response: Verdict
Recommended option: Recharter with changes (specifically tightening deliverable maturity expectations and fixing licensing/coordination/charter-quality details before or via AC comments; secondarily, a shorter charter with an explicit checkpoint is worth considering if generative-AI authoring guidance remains unincubated).
From the perspective of W3C as an institution and the wider web community, reviving the Authoring Tool Accessibility Guidelines Working Group (ATAG WG) after an 11-year closure is worthwhile in principle because authoring tools have a high leverage effect on web accessibility and AI-assisted content creation has shifted how content is authored. However, the proposed two-year charter ([https://www.w3.org/2026/11/proposed-atag-wg-charter.html](https://www.w3.org/2026/11/proposed-atag-wg-charter.html)) is under-scoped in commitment but over-scoped in licensing friction: it requests a full two-year Working Group with minimal staff allocation (0.09 FTE combined), names only a Working Draft as the target maturity for the "Next version of ATAG" with no draft existing today, leaves the W3C–Accessibility Standards Canada (ASC) Memorandum of Understanding (MOU) unverified in the charter text, and selects the W3C Document License for all deliverables—tightening from the ATAG Community Group's Software and Document License and risking blocks on joint work and external derivative standards.
1. Baseline & Pre-Charter History
Proposed charter: PROPOSED Authoring Tool Accessibility Guidelines Working Group Charter ([https://www.w3.org/2026/11/proposed-atag-wg-charter.html](https://www.w3.org/2026/11/proposed-atag-wg-charter.html), announced for AC review on public-new-work on 11 November 2026 through 9 December 2026). Duration: 2 years from Call for Participation. Chairs: Miriam Fukushima (W3C Invited Expert) and Wendy Reid (W3C Invited Expert). Team Contacts: Rémi Bétin (0.08 FTE) and Shawn Lawton Henry (0.01 FTE).
Prior WG history: The charter's "About this Charter" table lists an initial/previous charter period from 19 December 1997 to 5 November 2015, and the W3C group page ([https://www.w3.org/groups/wg/atag/](https://www.w3.org/groups/wg/atag/)) notes the ATAG WG closed on 15 December 2015 after completing ATAG 2.0 (published as a W3C Recommendation on 24 September 2015, alongside the Implementing ATAG 2.0 Working Group Note).
Prior charters & history verification gap: Direct retrieval of w3.org/groups/wg/atag/charters/ and w3.org/standards/history/atag/ was not returned cleanly in search/browse results; the 2010–2015 charter ([https://www.w3.org/WAI/AU/2010/auwg_charter.html](https://www.w3.org/WAI/AU/2010/auwg_charter.html)) shows prior charters in Dec 1997, Feb 1999, Jan 2000, Jun 2001, and Sep 2004, with extensions in 2014–2015. Exact intermediate charter dates and /standards/history/atag/ publication timestamps beyond ATAG 1.0 (3 Feb 2000) and ATAG 2.0 (24 Sep 2015) could not be independently verified from those two specific index URLs.
Community Group incubation: The Authoring Tools Accessibility Guidelines Community Group (ATAG CG) was proposed on 3 September 2025 and adopted its charter on 20 April 2026 ([https://w3c-cg.github.io/atag/ATAG_Charter.html](https://w3c-cg.github.io/atag/ATAG_Charter.html)), meeting regularly through late 2025 and 2026 to explore ATAG 2.0 updates and generative AI/LLM authoring issues.
What this charter buys & overlap: Rather than delivering a Recommendation in two years, the proposed charter's primary new normative deliverable ("Next version of ATAG") starts with no draft and targets only a Working Draft by Q2 2028 (FPWD), while editorially revising ATAG 2.0 and Implementing ATAG 2.0 in Q2 2027. Note that ATAG 2.0's maintenance/errata and alignment with WCAG 2.2 / WCAG 3 overlaps conceptually with the Accessibility Guidelines Working Group (AG WG) and the ATAG CG, and European accessibility requirements in ETSI EN 301 549 (V3.2.1 in clause 11.8; V4.1.1 published September 2026 moving authoring tool requirements to clause 5.10) already address authoring tool functions in a regulatory standard.
