2. Progress vs. Milestones
ATAG 2.0 & Implementing ATAG 2.0 editorial revision: Promised for Q2 2027 (explicitly caveated in the charter as "not meant as deadlines"). Because the WG has been closed since 2015 and only just restarted via the ATAG CG in 2025–2026, this is a forward milestone rather than a slipped one, though the charter's non-binding caveat weakens accountability.
Next version of ATAG: Promised as Use Cases & Requirements in Q3 2027 and an FPWD in Q2 2028, reaching only Working Draft status within the two-year charter window. The cause of having no draft today is that community incubation only formalized in April 2026 and generative AI / headless / API-driven authoring patterns are still being scoped.
Undated deliverables: Non-normative documents ("Use case and requirement documents", "Test suite and implementation report", "Primer or best practice documents") have no target dates, making progress unmeasurable. Moreover, a two-year charter whose flagship new normative deliverable only reaches Working Draft—and whose timeline explicitly states dates "are not meant as deadlines"—leaves milestone rigor weak.
3. Work Outside the Charter
The proposed charter sets Scope around updating ATAG 2.0 (including WCAG 2.2 references, clarifications, and outdated references), authoring tool/platform shifts (generative AI, API-driven, headless, and web-based platforms), evolving user needs, and accessible AI/automation-driven authoring tools, while placing Standards for the accessibility of content Out of Scope.
Active/adjacent work to watch against scope boundaries:
WCAG 3 alignment: The charter notes WCAG 3 is being developed and commits to liaising with AG WG on WCAG 2 and WCAG 3. Because WCAG 3 is still a Working Draft (expected CR not before late 2027), tying ATAG requirements to an unstable WCAG 3 draft risks scope creep or premature lock-in if the WG tries to define content-level accessibility rules rather than authoring-tool behaviors.
AI / LLM & agentic authoring: Both the ATAG CG charter and the proposed WG charter emphasize generative AI and LLMs. While "requirements and guidance for accessible AI- or automation-driven authoring tools" is inside Scope, W3C discussions in 2026 (including APA and Web & AI IG debates around AI training and agentic web interfaces) show fast-moving boundaries between authoring tools, AI models/agents, and end-user content quality.
Joint work with Accessibility Standards Canada (ASC): The charter states W3C and ASC "will work jointly to develop authoring tool accessibility guidelines" under an ASC–W3C MOU, whereas the Deliverables section lists W3C normative/non-normative outputs without detailing joint governance or deliverable ownership.
4. Real-World Adoption and Impact (Section 4b: Guidelines & Non-API Deliverables)
Because ATAG is a guidelines specification rather than a browser API, browser telemetry (web-features-explorer, caniuse, Chrome use counters, wpt.fyi) does not apply. Instead, Section 4b evaluates conformance claims, regulatory/procurement uptake, implementer engagement, successor/transition plans, evaluation tooling, and currency.
Conformance claims & tooling: ATAG 2.0 (2015) established Part A (accessible authoring tool UI) and Part B (supporting accessible content creation) mapped to WCAG 2.0, with W3C providing an ATAG Report Tool and Authoring Tools List. Public, independently verified ATAG conformance claims across mainstream CMSs, site builders, and AI authoring tools remain scarce in practice; many tools claim WCAG alignment or partial checks rather than full ATAG conformance reports.
Regulatory and procurement uptake: Authoring tool obligations appear in procurement and accessibility frameworks—such as U.S. Section 508 / IT accessibility guidance and European accessibility standards (ETSI EN 301 549, which included authoring tool requirements in clause 11.8 in V3.2.1 and updated them in V4.1.1 in September 2026)—giving authoring tool accessibility real policy relevance, even when ATAG 2.0 is referenced alongside or behind broader standards.
Implementer engagement & currency: ATAG 2.0 is 11 years old (published September 2015) and references WCAG 2.0, predating WCAG 2.1/2.2, WCAG 3, modern headless/block/API CMS architectures, and LLM/AI content generation. While the ATAG CG (formed Sept 2025, chartered Apr 2026) and Nov 2026 W3C outreach highlight authoring tools' "multiplier effect," broad participation from major CMS, LMS, no-code, and AI tool vendors is not yet demonstrated in the charter.
Value already delivered vs. next two years: ATAG 2.0 already delivered the conceptual Part A / Part B framework and 2015 Recommendation baseline. Two more years of WG work will not deliver a finished new Recommendation; at best, it will deliver an editorial refresh of ATAG 2.0 / Implementing ATAG 2.0 and a first Working Draft of the next version.
