6. Proposed Charter Quality & Licensing
Dates & milestones: Start and end dates are bracketed placeholders ([dd monthname yyyy]), which is standard prior to AC approval, and milestone dates (December 2026 through Q2 2028) are paired with a disclaimer that they "are not meant as deadlines." Non-normative deliverables have no dates.
Links, FTE, and Patent Policy: The charter cites the W3C Patent Policy (Version of 15 May 2025) and lists 0.09 FTE total (0.08 FTE Rémi Bétin + 0.01 FTE Shawn Lawton Henry), with chairs listed as W3C Invited Experts. Two Invited Experts as chairs on 0.09 FTE is thin staffing if active coordination with AG WG, Web & AI IG, horizontal groups, and ASC is expected.
Licensing audit (critical finding):
Proposed charter: The Licensing section states: "This Working Group will use the W3C Document license for all its deliverables." (The bottom footer of the proposed charter carries W3C's permissive document license link for the charter page itself.)
Comparison with ATAG CG charter & prior WG charter: The ATAG CG charter ([https://w3c-cg.github.io/atag/ATAG_Charter.html](https://w3c-cg.github.io/atag/ATAG_Charter.html), April 2026) specifies the W3C Software and Document License for specifications and states other documents should use that license where possible. Moving work from the ATAG CG into a WG under the W3C Document License therefore tightens the license on specifications and creates a potential mismatch for non-normative documents, test suites, or code/examples. Earlier ATAG WG charters (e.g., 2010 charter) focused on Patent Policy / Royalty-Free terms rather than a separate WG Document License clause.
Live draft check: Because the charter states there is no draft yet for the next version of ATAG, no new draft license notice can be inspected yet; ATAG 2.0 (2015) uses W3C's 2015 document use rules.
Concrete costs of the W3C Document License: The W3C Document License restricts creating derivative technical specifications, which can impede joint publication or specification reuse across W3C groups with different licensing terms, complicate adaptation or profiling by external standards bodies (such as Accessibility Standards Canada (ASC) under the cited ASC–W3C MOU, or ETSI / national standards bodies), and limit community/implementer reuse of primers, test materials, or examples. In addition, the charter references an ASC–W3C Memorandum of Understanding without confirming in the charter whether that MOU is signed, public, or addresses copyright/joint-specification terms.
7. Costs, Benefits, and Alternatives
Costs to W3C: Rather than dollar figures (which W3C does not publish per group), the costs are 0.09 FTE of W3C Team contact bandwidth, horizontal review and TAG oversight load as drafts mature, and reputational risk if a newly chartered WG stalls without a concrete draft or produces non-binding timelines.
What only W3C adds: Under the 15 May 2025 W3C Patent Policy, W3C Working Groups provide a formal Royalty-Free patent commitment framework (with formal exclusion opportunities tied to standards-track stages such as FPWD), formal horizontal review, and W3C Recommendation/Note status—advantages a Community Group alone cannot confer.
Participation health: The charter targets 6 or more active participants, including editors, test leads, and key implementers, with chairs, editors, and test leads expected to spend half a working day per week. Active CG participation in 2025–2026 exists, but broad authoring-tool and AI-vendor participation is not yet proven.
